00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:55.090
and then leveraging
a purpose-built tool

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:59.420
like Trifacta for
data preparation,

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.910
we want to get data into
the hands of more users

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.400
and let them be as
efficient as possible

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:05.990
in processing their data.

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:08.450
And that leads to data
preparation as a service.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:10.260
That's what Cloud
Dataprep is, right?

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:14.080
It allows you to visually
explore data, clean it,

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:17.149
structure it, whether
structure or unstructured data

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:19.400
or combine and modify
structured data,

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.149
with the whole goal
of making sure there's

00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:24.965
zero infrastructure, no
need to deploy and manage

00:04:24.965 --> 00:04:27.740
an environment,
and of course, not

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.960
needing to know how to code.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:31.710
CORI PEELE: Great.

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:34.170
SEAN MA: So what does this
fit inside the data lifecycle?

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:37.590
So as you can see here, this is
a typical lifecycle for anyone

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:38.640
who works with data.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.532
You take raw data
upfront, and then you're

00:04:40.532 --> 00:04:42.240
going through a kind
of a discovery mode,

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.890
understanding what's
inside your data,

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.770
structuring that, cleansing
it, followed by enriching it,

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:48.690
combining with
other data sources,

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.874
validating it to make sure
it meets what you need.

00:04:50.874 --> 00:04:52.290
And this is a very
iterative loop.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:54.373
This is something that you
might do multiple times

00:04:54.373 --> 00:04:58.110
and go forth from there before
you decide that you've prepared

00:04:58.110 --> 00:04:59.550
the data or what you need.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:01.830
You automate that so
you can do it repeatedly

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.350
for downstream analytics or
some sort of machine learning

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:05.760
or modeling.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.890
Now, the goal with Dataprep is
to make this process here as

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:14.530
efficient as possible and
reduce the time you spend on it.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:17.550
So for example, instead of it
normally taking months or weeks

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:19.680
to go to solve this
problem and go through all

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.410
the different steps, we really
want to make it days and hours.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:23.970
So that's one part
of the efficiency.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:27.720
The other part is actually to
enable different types of users

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:29.350
to actually access the data.

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:31.960
So this is not just for data
scientists or data engineers,

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:34.860
but also analysts, people who
are actually using the end

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:36.240
results of these analytics.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.310
We want them to
have access to tools

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:39.561
that can prepare data as well.

00:05:39.561 --> 00:05:40.310
CORI PEELE: Great.

