00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:39.600
SEAN MA: Right.

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:40.720
Yeah.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.870
So let's move on to some
applications and use cases

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:45.290
that we've seen Dataprep for.

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:48.210
At a high level, some of the
top applications that we've

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.430
seen here are data onboarding.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:54.870
This is a scenario where
we see analysts dealing

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:57.180
with third-party data,
typically data coming

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:00.810
from external vendors
or human-generated data,

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.880
where there's a lot of
information in that data,

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.680
but you need to
standardize it, and you

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:06.660
need to restructure
the data in order

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:08.730
to meet internal
standards so that it can

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:10.750
be fed into built-in pipelines.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:14.505
You can fill it into
reports down the stream.

00:07:14.505 --> 00:07:16.880
The next one is data science
and machine learning, right?

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:20.310
So data prep is not just
a challenge for analysts.

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:22.470
It's also for data
scientists, because what's

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:25.170
the point of training a
model on a particular feature

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:27.000
if the quality of
the data is poor?

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.560
That can lead to
very skewed results.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:28.950
CORI PEELE: Yeah.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.030
SEAN MA: So making sure that
you've got the right data,

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:32.450
you isolate the right features.

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:34.350
Feeding that into the
model is important.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:36.724
The last one is actually the
most common one we've heard,

00:07:36.724 --> 00:07:38.460
which is big data
analytics, right?

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:40.320
And the challenge of
big data analytics

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:42.750
is that now, as more
and more data is

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:44.820
available of different
formats, it's

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:50.280
not just about processing
traditional or relational data,

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:52.830
it's actually combining
that data with social media,

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:55.320
combining that data with
file data, clickstream data.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.737
So you can get a
very holistic view

00:07:57.737 --> 00:07:59.320
of what's going on
in your environment

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.490
and analyze data based on that.

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:03.540
CORI PEELE: Great.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:04.140
Yeah.

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:06.120
So when you're
looking at this, you

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:08.220
have a lot of different options.

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:10.350
You support a lot of
different use cases.

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:13.470
And in particular, when
we're thinking about,

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:16.140
like you mentioned,
machine learning, so let's

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:18.600
talk a little bit about how
companies are leveraging

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:20.850
data prep for machine
learning models

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.400
and what you need to do
before you start your feature

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:23.940
engineering.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:24.523
SEAN MA: Yeah.

00:08:24.523 --> 00:08:28.859
So I can give you an example
of a health care company.

00:08:28.859 --> 00:08:30.900
So they actually started
with actually analytics.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:32.210
So their challenge was--

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:34.200
so this is a large
health care provider.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.900
They have dozens of
hospitals, hundreds

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:40.200
of outpatient facilities
throughout the United States.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.480
And their challenge
was understanding

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:46.890
the resources, both people,
medicines, machine's, resources

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:51.600
across all these locations,
understanding what's there

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.807
and how the inventory
was stocked across that.

00:08:54.807 --> 00:08:57.390
So the first challenge was just
gathering all this information

00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:01.430
from the various systems,
traditional systems, customer--

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:03.360
user-generated
information, gathering it

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:06.780
in a single location just to
get a good picture of what's

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:07.660
going on.

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:09.630
And they were able to
do that through big data

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:11.130
analytics using
big data analytics,

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:13.170
because they could
see holistically

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:15.400
all of the datasets combined
and blended together.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:15.780
CORI PEELE: Great.

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:18.404
SEAN MA: But once they had that,
what was interesting about it,

00:09:18.404 --> 00:09:21.450
was that same data was then
useful for actually feeding

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:25.450
into a machine learning model.

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:27.390
In this case, it was
a flu predictor model.

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:29.460
So what they could do
was they could actually

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:32.730
use the information from
where inventory was, where

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:35.310
it was being used up, to
help feed into a model

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:37.050
and predict where
there was most likely

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:38.790
going to be a flu
outbreak, because they

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:39.690
saw inventories dip.

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:42.120
They saw patterns coming in.

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:45.090
So that's actually a case where
big data analytics eventually

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.490
fed into a machine
learning use case.

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:50.910
And they were able to deploy
that and to the benefit of all.

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:53.041
CORI PEELE: That's great.

00:09:53.041 --> 00:09:53.540
SEAN MA: OK.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:55.767
So these are some
of the use cases.

00:09:55.767 --> 00:09:57.350
So these are some
of the applications.

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:59.183
Let's talk about some
of the tasks and users

00:09:59.183 --> 00:10:01.580
that we've seen with data prep.

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:03.020
I mentioned this
before, but it's

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:05.500
a fairly wide range
of users that we've

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:08.060
seen with Cloud Dataprep,
from data scientists, pretty

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:09.860
much anyone who uses
data, whether they're

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:11.554
analysts or engineers.

00:10:11.554 --> 00:10:12.970
And as you can see
here, there are

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:15.480
a set of tasks that
are common across them.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:18.500
So those include formatting,
restructuring, getting data

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:24.080
into a format that you will
understand so you can do things

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:26.120
with it, then
profiling that data

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:28.670
to get a sense of the
shape of the data itself.

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:30.590
So you can understand
the characteristics,

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:34.610
how clean it is, what are some
of the potential anomalies

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:35.660
inside that data.

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:37.160
And then universally
what we've seen

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:39.066
is that no matter what,
the data is dirty and needs

00:10:39.066 --> 00:10:39.800
to be cleansed.

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:43.086
So standardizing it, fixing
out the common issues.

00:10:43.086 --> 00:10:44.710
So that's kind of
like the common tasks

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:46.490
that we've seen across all.

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:49.250
Then we've seen data
scientists, in particular,

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:50.990
but to some degree
data engineers,

00:10:50.990 --> 00:10:53.990
focusing on pattern
identification and distribution

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:56.320
models as well as,
as I mentioned,

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:01.370
training ML models
for predictions

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:02.850
and usage down the load.

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:06.099
So that's kind of where we see a
lot of pattern, data scientists

00:11:06.099 --> 00:11:06.890
and data engineers.

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:08.723
Now, what's interesting
is that after you've

00:11:08.723 --> 00:11:11.120
got the data in the
format that you want,

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:15.010
everyone wants to do some
sort of ad hoc analysis.

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:17.660
Now, if you understand the data
better, you can explore better,

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:19.809
you can find new
insights inside of that.

00:11:19.809 --> 00:11:22.100
And once you do, you typically
want to enrich the data,

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:24.020
combine it with
other data sources,

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:25.940
and with the end
goal for analysts,

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:29.850
in particular, to visualize it
in some sort of dashboarding UI

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:32.960
or BI capability.

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:36.350
CORI PEELE: So how does data
preparation differ from ETL?

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:38.300
Because some of
these common themes

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:41.150
around enrichment and
cleansing are often

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:43.187
a part of that type
of process as well.

00:11:43.187 --> 00:11:43.770
SEAN MA: Yeah.

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:46.430
It's a great question, something
that we get quite often.

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:47.960
So it's not black and white.

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:50.600
In fact, oftentimes
these two concepts

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:52.730
are complementary to
each other, right?

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:56.810
Typically, ETL is focusing on
well-defined structured data

00:11:56.810 --> 00:12:00.435
while data prep is handling
unstructured, complex data

00:12:00.435 --> 00:12:02.910
that's coming in from
non-relational sources.

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:04.650
So that's one area.

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:06.500
But as part of
that, you also see

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:09.320
a difference in the style
in which the tools behave.

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:12.500
So data prep solutions
typically combine

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:14.690
visualization with
machine learning,

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:17.390
all put together into an
interactive user interface

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:19.190
so that you can
access multiple users,

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:22.760
while ETL is focusing on a
metadata-driven mapping that

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:25.350
connects kind of a
flow through the data.

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:28.310
So these two are complementary,
but they're really targeting

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:29.642
different types of users.

00:12:29.642 --> 00:12:31.100
And then I'd say
one key difference

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:33.860
that you might choose is
that the time to insight.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:37.490
In particular, if you're
dealing with ad hoc data or data

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:39.200
sources that are
changing quite a bit,

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:41.270
you may want to use
data prep, because it

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:44.010
has a little bit more
flexibility for these changes

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:45.240
and go forward from there.

